$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{A}_{1}+{x}_{2}{A}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167580 #edb/ref 1061 653397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e61da532-1350-59ec-be76-0a0464423ad6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167581 #edb/ref 1061 653398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a4ae83d-c5c3-5b9f-bd08-97b2e0bc398a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167582 #edb/ref 1061 653401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e896c1e-d15c-5ab8-ab0f-39877293d9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167583 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167583 #edb/ref 1061 653404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b14a3b6-adad-5d10-bce3-5e05e7cf5144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167584 #edb/ref 1061 653407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167584 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f13dd7dd-f256-5871-9c5b-37fcd35b85a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167584 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168064 #edb/ref 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168064 #edb/ref 1065 4962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168064 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6638787f-3ef3-5623-bcec-de4b89ebfd02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168064 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168065 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we fix a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with scalars from $\\mathbb{R}$ (note that \"finite-dimensional\" means that the basis contains a finite number of elements). Then, we can represent any element $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ using the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c9297a30f87baf08295a752cb23f2d314139508f875b4ffa2fffc8763656bd54.svg)\n\nThis defines a bijective mapping $\\phi: V\\to \\mathbb{R}^n.$ Moreover, this particular mapping has some nice properties:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}) = \\phi(\\mathbf{u}) + \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in V,$ and\n\n- $\\phi(\\alpha \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nBecause of this, we say that $V$ is **isomorphic** to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Intuitively, this means that $V$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have identical structures.\n\nSo, any finite-dimensional vector space is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some particular $n.$ This means that instead of studying all possible vector spaces, we can simply study only one family of vector spaces, namely $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168065 #edb/ref 1065 4964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168065 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e501b24f-7ea0-5110-a4c5-e8739ea9ac11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168065 #edb/ref 1067 "Isomorphisms of Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241363 #edb/ref 1080 "-2{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241363 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241363 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15301301-2403-50d4-a624-ab391b2dc478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241667 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "376486a2-8e17-5715-a7ad-737fa78c1258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241840 #edb/ref 1080 "-2-2t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241840 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241840 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48d12868-f91f-5e4d-be31-2ca61761e838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242278 #edb/ref 1080 "2+2t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242278 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d679e70-9da7-5202-a81d-707bd1efa619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242337 #edb/ref 1080 "{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242337 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242337 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8468e60b-9874-537b-9d9b-53bca61026d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242439 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242439 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242439 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f04b25d-f38d-5fc1-9876-423c80368bba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242724 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b15931fb-52e5-5c41-a258-369e7d1fb1c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242754 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242754 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242754 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b42567a6-9890-5794-8a22-d6d56c1dc629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243013 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243013 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243013 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d0121691-eca2-5974-80e3-dbeade97f6d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243380 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9e06cb0-4838-5459-9758-b2749164bf96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186242724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243839 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e685e6a5-808d-5d60-91f6-26ddb332e292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243879 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd008c33-82db-5fa0-8507-c0d8e860acf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243941 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+t+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186243941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33e006b4-f466-5a45-99fb-a4284803e97a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243941 #edb/ref 1074 "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[p(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =(-1)\\cdot (1+t+{t}^{2})+1\\cdot (1+t-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-1-t-{t}^{2}+1+t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186243954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243954 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=6+2t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186243954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dfc56d2-f4d4-58d9-9174-ebcc9a95025f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243954 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t+2{t}^{2}) & =6+2t \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =6+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$  Substituting this into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+(-2)=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186357943 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357943 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357943 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15ad0a48-81fc-5240-8a93-2ba2c02e3c92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357961 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357961 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357961 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "180fb67f-6a67-5314-a5a7-83e4f4288271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358051 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358051 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358051 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22ad037a-f496-5ab0-a932-5d46d0a21741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358084 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358084 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358084 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25191c8e-2e57-514d-a0e3-97eb11b38614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358131 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358131 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358131 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "298460b6-9c2f-56b8-8ced-e7d6ae2bf14d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358442 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358442 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921